---
import Document from "@/layouts/Document.astro";
import { currentRevision } from "@/lib/atlas";
import { getDataset } from "@/lib/data";
import { passiveChecks } from "@/lib/scanner";

const data = getDataset();
const revision = currentRevision(data);
const scannerChecks = passiveChecks(data).length;
const repo = "https://github.com/letsauthlab/letsauth";
const issue = `${repo}/issues/new?template=observation.yml`;
const entry = `- server: stripe                     # must equal the file's server id
  feature: mcp.as.cimd               # a feature with party mcp-server or authorization-server
  revision: { spec: mcp-authorization, revision: "${revision.id}" }
  status: supported                  # supported | partial | unsupported | unknown | not-applicable
  method: passive-scan               # manual | vendor-claim | passive-scan | active-scan
  observedAt: 2026-10-13T18:40:00Z
  observedBy: jbspeakr               # your handle, or scanner@<version>
  evidence: https://auth.stripe.com/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server`;
---

<Document
  title="Contribute"
  heading="Add what you know"
  description="How to add an observation, correct a cell, list a server or withdraw one from the MCP auth atlas. A maintainer reviews every change before it is published."
  path="/contribute/"
  eyebrow="Contribute"
  lede="The atlas is a dataset in a git repository. You can add an observation or correct one, with evidence, and a maintainer reviews it before it goes live."
>
  <h2>Two ways in</h2>
  <p>
    Pick the one that fits what you have. Both end in a reviewed pull request, and the result
    looks the same in the atlas.
  </p>
  <ul class="rows" role="list">
    <li>
      <p class="eyebrow">Path A</p>
      <h3 class="rows__title">Open an issue</h3>
      <p>
        This is the quickest route. The form asks for the same fields as a data entry: server,
        feature, revision, status, method, date, evidence and your handle. A maintainer turns an
        accepted issue into a pull request. When the issue itself is the evidence, the entry
        links to it.
      </p>
      <p><a class="button" href={issue} rel="noopener">Open the issue form</a></p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="eyebrow">Path B</p>
      <h3 class="rows__title">Open a pull request</h3>
      <p>
        You append an entry to the server's observation file yourself. The checks run on your
        machine and again in CI, so a reviewer only has to open the evidence. The steps are
        below.
      </p>
      <p><a class="button quiet" href={repo} rel="noopener">Open the repository</a></p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The pull request, step by step</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      Append an entry to <code>data/observations/&lt;server-id&gt;.yaml</code>. Create the file if
      the server has none.
    </li>
    <li>
      Run <code>pnpm validate:data</code>. Failures name the file, the entry index and the field,
      for example <code>data/observations/stripe.yaml[3].evidence: required (URL or note)</code>.
    </li>
    <li>Open the pull request. CI repeats the validation and the append-only check.</li>
    <li>A maintainer reviews the evidence and merges. The next deploy publishes it.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Entry format</h3>
  <pre tabindex="0"><code>{entry}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <strong>Evidence</strong> is a URL a reviewer can open, such as a metadata document, a
    documentation page or a response capture. A precise note works too.
    <strong>Method</strong> says how you know. Use <code>manual</code> for something you checked
    by hand, <code>vendor-claim</code> for what the vendor says, <code>passive-scan</code> for
    reading public metadata and <code>active-scan</code> for sending requests.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a cell has two observations, the atlas shows the newest. If both have the same time,
    the stronger method wins: active-scan, then passive-scan, then manual, then vendor-claim.
  </p>

  <h2>Let the scanner write it</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/mcp/scanner/">passive scanner</a> can read {scannerChecks} requirements from
    public metadata. For those, run <code>pnpm scan &lt;server-id&gt; --write</code> instead of
    writing the entry by hand. It appends only the observations whose status or evidence changed,
    and anyone can repeat the same command to check them. The nightly run does this for every
    listed server and opens its own pull request.
  </p>

  <h2>The append-only rule</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    <p class="callout__title">Observations are never edited or removed</p>
    <p>
      A correction is a new entry with a newer <code>observedAt</code>. The old one stays in the
      history on the server page. CI fails any pull request that changes or deletes an existing
      entry.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Listing a server</h2>
  <p>
    Add <code>data/servers/&lt;id&gt;.yaml</code> with the endpoint, the transport and
    <code>listed</code>. If you maintain the server, use <code>listed: opt-in</code>. If not,
    give the <code>source</code> that lists it publicly. We list servers as they appear in public
    directories, and we score or rank nobody.
  </p>

  <h2>Withdrawing a server</h2>
  <p>
    If you maintain a server and do not want it listed, use the issue form. Choose "withdraw my
    server" and give us a way to check that you maintain it. A pull request then sets
    <code>withdrawn</code> on the server. It leaves the matrix and the export, and its
    observations stay in the repository history.
  </p>

  <h2>Licences</h2>
  <p>
    Data you contribute is published under <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/LICENSE-DATA`} rel="noopener">CC BY 4.0</a>.
    The site's code is <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/LICENSE`} rel="noopener">MIT</a>.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="aside">
    <div class="panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">Quick links</p>
      <ul class="links" role="list">
        <li><a href={issue} rel="noopener">Issue form</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${repo}/tree/main/data/observations`} rel="noopener">Observation files</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/atlas-maintenance.md`} rel="noopener">Maintenance guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/mcp/data.json">The atlas as JSON</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</Document>

<style>
  .links {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--small);
  }
  .links a {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .links a:hover {
    color: var(--signal);
  }
</style>
